(function(){window.PhysicaRankPyqNeet_zoology_evolution=[{id:"neet-2026-q140",pn:140,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 3 May",tp:"Evolution",st:"Human evolution",q:"Evolution of human appears parallel to the progressive development of brain and language skills. As such, the evolution of individual species in the sequence of their appearance is:",o:["Homo habilis \u2192 Homo erectus \u2192 Ramapithecus \u2192 Neanderthal \u2192 Homo sapiens","Ramapithecus \u2192 Homo habilis \u2192 Homo erectus \u2192 Neanderthal \u2192 Homo sapiens","Homo sapiens \u2192 Ramapithecus \u2192 Homo habilis \u2192 Neanderthal \u2192 Homo erectus","Neanderthal \u2192 Ramapithecus \u2192 Homo habilis \u2192 Homo erectus \u2192 Homo sapiens"],c:1,s:`Human evolution appears parallel to the progressive development of brain and language skills.
The correct chronological order is Ramapithecus \u2192 Australopithecines \u2192 Homo habilis \u2192 Homo erectus \u2192 Neanderthalensis \u2192 Homo sapiens.
So option (2) is correct.`},{id:"neet-2026r-q149",pn:149,y:"Re-NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun",tp:"Evolution",st:"Homologous organs",q:`Given below are two statements : one is labelled as Assertion A and the other is labelled as Reason R.
Assertion A : Forelimbs of human and bats are homologous.
Reason R : Forelimbs of humans and bats have similar anatomical structure.
In the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below.`,o:["Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true","Both A and R are correct and R is the correct explanation of A"],c:3,s:`Forelimbs of bats and humans have a similar pattern of bone arrangement.
They do different functions but have similar anatomical structure: humerus, radius, ulna, carpals, metacarpals and phalanges.
The same structure developed in different directions due to adaptation to different needs.
This is divergent evolution, so these structures are homologous.`},{id:"neet-2026r-q165",pn:165,y:"Re-NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun",tp:"Evolution",st:"Hardy-Weinberg principle",q:"A population of diploid organisms is at Hardy-Weinberg equilibrium. If the frequency of allele A is 0.1, the frequency of AA is ________.",o:["0.02","0.10","0.99","0.01"],c:3,s:`Frequency of allele A, p = 0.1.
At Hardy-Weinberg equilibrium, frequency of AA = p\xB2.
p\xB2 = (0.1)\xB2 = 0.01.`},{id:"neet-2026r-q166",pn:166,y:"Re-NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun",tp:"Evolution",st:"Adaptive radiation",q:"Adaptive radiation in placental mammals and Australian Marsupials leading to similarity between distant species is an example of ________.",o:["convergent evolution","founder effect","genetic drift","divergent evolution"],c:0,s:`When more than one adaptive radiation appears in an isolated geographical area (representing different habitats), it is called convergent evolution.
Placental mammals in Australia also show adaptive radiation into varieties.
Each of them looks similar to a corresponding marsupial.`},{id:"neet-2026r-q170",pn:170,y:"Re-NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun",tp:"Evolution",st:"Natural selection",q:`Natural selection can lead to ________.
(a) stabilisation
(b) genetic drift
(c) directional change
(d) disruption
Choose the correct answer from the options given below:`,o:["(a), (c) and (d) only","(a), (b), (c) and (d)","(a) and (c) only","(a) only"],c:0,s:`Natural selection can lead to stabilisation, directional change or disruption.
In natural selection, heritable variations that help better survival let organisms reproduce and leave more progeny.
Variation from mutation, recombination, gene flow or genetic drift changes the frequency of genes and alleles in the next generation.`},{id:"neet-2026r-q174",pn:174,y:"Re-NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun",tp:"Evolution",st:"Evidences for evolution",q:"Which of the following is not evidence for evolution?",o:["Paleontological evidence from fossil records","Embryological support for evolution as proposed by Ernst Heckel","Divergent evolution of anatomical structures such as forelimbs","Convergent evolution of traits like wings of birds and butterflies"],a:"2/4*",s:`Divergent evolution of structures such as forelimbs shows homology.
Homology shows common ancestry and is evidence for evolution.
When the same structure develops in different directions for different needs, the structures are homologous.
Convergent evolution is not evidence, because species can develop similar features without a common ancestor.
Fossils are remains of hard parts of life forms found in rocks.
Studying fossils in different sedimentary layers shows the geological period in which they lived.
Embryological support was proposed by Ernst Heckel from features common to vertebrate embryos but absent in adults.
Later this proposal was disproved by Karl Ernst von Baer.
Note: Printed answer is '2/4*' (two options, 2 or 4, with an asterisk); solution supports both: Heckel's proposal was disapproved by von Baer, and convergent evolution is not evidence of common ancestry.`},{id:"neet-2026-q174",pn:174,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 3 May",tp:"Evolution",st:"Convergent evolution",q:"Which of the following is not an example of convergent evolution?",o:["Eyes of octopuses and mammals","Fore limbs of whales and bats","Wings of butterflies and birds","Flippers of penguins and dolphins"],c:1,s:"Fore limbs of whales and bats are examples of divergent evolution and show homology."},{id:"neet-2026r-q176",pn:176,y:"Re-NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun",tp:"Evolution",st:"Origin of man",q:`Given below are two statements:
Statement I: Modern Homo sapiens arose in Australia and moved across continents.
Statement II: Homo sapiens arose around 75000 to 10000 years ago.
In the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:`,o:["Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are correct"],c:2,s:`Homo sapiens arose in Africa and moved across continents, developing into distinct races. So Statement I is incorrect.
Modern Homo sapiens arose during the ice age, between 75,000 and 10,000 years ago. So Statement II is correct.`},{id:"neet-2026-q179",pn:179,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 3 May",tp:"Evolution",st:"History of life on earth",q:`Match List I with List II with respect to chronology of evolution of life forms
A. About 65 mya \u2014 I. Jawless fish probably evolved
B. About 500 mya \u2014 II. The dinosaurs suddenly disappeared from the earth
C. About 350 mya \u2014 III. Seaweeds and few plants probably existed
D. About 320 mya \u2014 IV. Invertebrates were formed and became active`,o:["A(II), B(IV), C(I), D(III)","A(II), B(IV), C(III), D(I)","A(I), B(II), C(III), D(IV)","A(III), B(IV), C(I), D(II)"],c:0,s:`About 65 mya: the dinosaurs suddenly disappeared from the earth (A-II).
About 500 mya: invertebrates were formed and became active (B-IV).
About 350 mya: jawless fish probably evolved (C-I).
About 320 mya: seaweeds and few plants probably existed (D-III).
So A-II, B-IV, C-I, D-III.`},{id:"neet-2025-q096",pn:96,y:"NEET 2025",tp:"Evolution",st:"RNA world, origin of DNA",q:`Given below are two statements :
Statement I : In the RNA world, RNA is considered the first genetic material evolved to carry out essential life processes. RNA acts as a genetic material and also as a catalyst for some important biochemical reactions in living systems. Being reactive, RNA is unstable.
Statement II : DNA evolved from RNA and is a more stable genetic material. Its double helical strands being complementary, resist changes by evolving repairing mechanism.
In the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below :`,o:["Both statement I and statement II are correct","Both statement I and statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but statement II is correct"],c:0,s:`In the RNA world, RNA was the first genetic material.
There is enough evidence that essential life processes (metabolism, translation, splicing, etc.) evolved around RNA.
RNA acted as genetic material and also as a catalyst; some biochemical reactions are catalysed by RNA catalysts, not by protein enzymes. So statement I is correct.
Statement II is also correct: DNA is double stranded with complementary strands, and it resists changes by evolving a repair process.`},{id:"neet-2025-q151",pn:151,y:"NEET 2025",tp:"Evolution",st:"Homology and analogy",q:"Sweet potato and potato represent a certain type of evolution. Select the correct combination of terms to explain the evolution.",o:["Analogy, convergent","Homology, divergent","Homology, convergent","Analogy, divergent"],c:0,s:`Sweet potato is a root modification while potato is a stem modification, but both have the same function.
Analogous structures are not anatomically similar but perform similar functions.
Analogous structures are the result of convergent evolution.
Homologous organs are anatomically similar but do not perform similar functions.
Homologous organs are the result of divergent evolution.`},{id:"neet-2024-q162",pn:162,y:"NEET 2024 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Evolution",st:"Convergent evolution",q:"The flippers of the Penguins and Dolphins are the example of the",o:["Convergent evolution","Divergent evolution","Adaptive radiation","Natural selection"],c:0,s:`Option (1) is correct.
The flippers do the same job but the structures are not anatomically alike, so they are analogous structures.
Divergent evolution gives homologous structures, so (2) is wrong.
Adaptive radiation is evolution of different species from one point radiating to other areas, so (3) is wrong.
Natural selection is a mechanism of evolution, so (4) is wrong.`},{id:"neet-2024-q164",pn:164,y:"NEET 2024 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Evolution",st:"Hardy-Weinberg principle",q:"Which one of the following factors will not affect the Hardy-Weinberg equilibrium?",o:["Gene migration","Constant gene pool","Genetic recombination","Genetic drift"],c:1,s:`Option (2) is correct.
A constant gene pool does not disturb Hardy-Weinberg equilibrium.
Gene migration, genetic recombination and genetic drift disturb it and lead to evolution.`},{id:"neet-2024-q165",pn:165,y:"NEET 2024 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Evolution",st:"Human evolution",q:`Given below are some stages of human evolution. Arrange them in correct sequence. (Past to Recent)
A. Homo habilis
B. Homo sapiens
C. Homo neanderthalensis
D. Homo erectus
Choose the correct sequence of human evolution from the options given below :`,o:["C-B-D-A","A-D-C-B","D-A-C-B","B-A-D-C"],c:1,s:`Option (2) is correct.
From past to recent: Homo habilis \u2192 Homo erectus \u2192 Homo neanderthalensis \u2192 Homo sapiens.`},{id:"neet-2024-q187",pn:187,y:"NEET 2024 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Evolution",st:"Geological time scale",q:`Match List I with List II:
A. Mesozoic Era \u2014 I. Lower invertebrates
B. Proterozoic Era \u2014 II. Fish & Amphibia
C. Cenozoic Era \u2014 III. Birds & Reptiles
D. Paleozoic Era \u2014 IV. Mammals
Choose the correct answer from the options given below:`,o:["A-I, B-II, C-IV, D-III","A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II","A-II, B-I, C-III, D-IV","A-III, B-I, C-II, D-IV"],c:1,s:`Mesozoic Era \u2013 III. Birds & Reptiles.
Proterozoic Era \u2013 I. Lower invertebrates.
Cenozoic Era \u2013 IV. Mammals.
Paleozoic Era \u2013 II. Fish & Amphibia.
This gives A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II.`}]})();
